.TH "build-apt-repo" "8" "2026-09-11" "ltsp6"
.SH "NAME"
build\-apt\-repo \(em Build and sign the apt archive published over GitHub Pages
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
\fBbuild\-apt\-repo\fR \fIrepo\-dir\fR [\fIsuite\fR \fIpackage.deb\fR ...]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
Writes conf/distributions with \fBSignWith\fR, includes any packages given,
then exports and signs every suite and verifies its own output before
finishing. The archive lives on an orphan branch of the source repository and
is served over HTTPS by GitHub Pages.
.PP
Called with only \fIrepo\-dir\fR it re\-exports and re\-signs what is already
there, which is what an expiring key or a changed suite list needs.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP
.IP "\fIrepo\-dir\fP" 4
A checkout of the archive branch.
.IP "\fIsuite\fP \fIpackage.deb\fP ..." 4
Optional. One suite followed by one or more packages to include in it.
\fBincludedeb\fR replaces an existing version of the same package in that
suite, so re\-running with a rebuilt package is the normal update path.
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.PP
.IP "\fBGNUPGHOME\fP" 4
Where the signing key lives. Defaults to the caller's ~/.gnupg.
.IP "\fBLTSP6_REPO_KEY\fP" 4
Fingerprint of the key to sign with. Without it, the single available secret
key is used.
.SH "EXAMPLE"
.PP
.nf
git clone \-b main https://github.com/MAButz/ltsp6.git ltsp6\-apt
build\-apt\-repo ltsp6\-apt trixie ../ltsp6\-session\-tuning_1.0.0_all.deb
cd ltsp6\-apt && git add \-A && git commit \-m "publish ..." && git push
.fi
.SH "CLIENT SIDE"
.PP
.nf
install \-d \-m 755 /etc/apt/keyrings
curl \-fsSL \-o /etc/apt/keyrings/ltsp6.asc \
    https://mabutz.github.io/ltsp6/ltsp6\-archive\-keyring.asc

cat > /etc/apt/sources.list.d/ltsp6.sources <<'SRC'
Types: deb
URIs: https://mabutz.github.io/ltsp6
Suites: trixie
Components: main
Architectures: amd64
Signed\-By: /etc/apt/keyrings/ltsp6.asc
SRC
.fi
.PP
Both steps are required. Without the key apt refuses the archive, and the only
way around that is \fB[trusted=yes]\fR, which switches off verification for
every package from the source. On machines that install software as root that
is not a corner worth cutting. A correct \fBapt update\fR fetches InRelease and
says nothing about signatures.
.SH "NOTES"
.PP
\fBSignWith\fR is the whole point of conf/distributions. Without that line the
export succeeds and produces an archive no client will trust \(em there is no
error, just a missing InRelease.
.PP
The archive branch needs \fB.gitattributes\fR containing \fB* \-text\fR. An apt
archive is verified byte for byte: Release lists the checksums of Packages, and
InRelease carries a signature over exactly those bytes. A line\-ending
conversion anywhere in the chain \(em on checkout, on a colleague's machine with
\fBcore.autocrlf=true\fR, in CI on Windows \(em produces a hash mismatch that
reads like a broken mirror rather than a git setting.
.PP
\fB.nojekyll\fR keeps GitHub Pages from running Jekyll over checksummed files.
.PP
reprepro's db/ is local state rebuilt from conf/ and pool/, not source.
Committing a Berkeley DB adds a binary blob on every publish for no gain.
.PP
The private half of the signing key must never be committed. A signing key that
has ever been pushed has to be replaced, not deleted in a follow\-up commit.
.PP
Every .deb committed to the archive branch stays in the git history forever, so
each rebuild of a large package adds another full copy. GitHub Pages publishes
at most 1 GB per site. For anything sizeable, publish a release asset instead
and keep the archive for small packages.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
\fBreprepro\fP(1),
\fBapt\-secure\fP(8),
\fBsources.list\fP(5),
\fBgpg\fP(1).
.SH "AVAILABILITY"
.PP
\fBbuild\-apt\-repo\fR is part of the \fBltsp\-server\fP package; the latest
version is available from \fIhttps://github.com/MAButz/ltsp6\fR.
